    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "From adjective to noun",
     "texto": [
      "At this level you talk about qualities as things, so you need the nouns. Each ending has its pattern:\n\n- **-dad**: generoso → **la generosidad**; sincero → **la sinceridad**\n- **-ez**: tímido → **la timidez**; maduro → **la madurez**\n- **-ía**: valiente → **la valentía**; cobarde → **la cobardía**\n- **-ura**: tierno → **la ternura**\n- **-ismo**: egoísta → **el egoísmo**\n- **-ción / -cia**: ambicioso → **la ambición**; arrogante → **la arrogancia**\n\n**The opposite** is formed with **in-**, **im-** or **ir-**: **inconstante**, **impuntual**, **irresponsable**.\n\n**Ser or tener.** **Es valiente** = **tiene valentía**.\n\n**False friend.** In Spanish **sensible** is someone who feels a lot; someone who thinks wisely is **sensato**.\n\n**Colourful idioms.** **No tiene dos dedos de frente**: he has little sense. **Está como una cabra**: he is mad, said fondly. **Es un trozo de pan**: he is very kind."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Qualities: from adjective to noun",
     "texto": [
      "**The noun of a quality** comes from the adjective with an ending:\n\n- **-dad** and **-ez**: **la generosidad**, **la timidez**\n- **-ía**: **la valentía**, **la cobardía**\n- **-ción** and **-cia**: **la ambición**, **la arrogancia**\n\nThey are all feminine, except those ending in **-ismo** (**el egoísmo**).\n\n**The opposite** takes **in-**, **im-** or **ir-**. Before **r** the prefix is **ir-**: **irresponsable**.\n\n**Ser** + adjective equals **tener** + noun: **es valiente** = **tiene valentía**."
     ],
